## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of approximate quantiles with streaming data, a common constraint in production systems. It compares simple histograms to exact tracking and asks for concrete complexity and error bounds.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms for quantiles
- Per-element O(1) updates, O(W) queries
- Error bounds from bucket width w

## Code Example
```javascript
function HistQuantile(m, W) {
  const bucket = new Array(W).fill(0);
  this.n = 0; this.m = m; this.W = W;
  const w = Math.ceil(m / W);
  this.update = (x) => { const idx = Math.min(Math.floor(x / w), W-1); bucket[idx]++; this.n++; };
  this.median = () => {
    let target = Math.floor(this.n / 2) + 1;
    let cum = 0; let i = 0; const bw = Math.ceil(m / W);
    for (; i < W; i++) { if (cum + bucket[i] >= target) break; cum += bucket[i]; }
    const L = i * bw; const left = target - cum;
    const est = left <= bucket[i] ? L + (left / bucket[i]) * bw : L + bw;
    return est;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for skewed data?  
- How does choosing W affect error vs memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

